    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1040">

                <pb facs="1174" n="1164" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg1164"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94114 Mail/57: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-27T19:00:00+03:00">March 27, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 28—11:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1072. Foreign Office states that the 11 sacks of prisoners of war mail mentioned
                    in Embassy’s 678 [<hi rendition="#italic">687</hi>], February 29, 11
                        p.m.,<anchor n="74" xml:id="d1040-f1"/> plus 10 additional sacks
                    subsequently received from Tehran have been turned over to the Commissariat of
                    Communications and will be forwarded to their destination in the near future. An
                    early reply on the general question of rerouting American prisoner of war mail
                    (reference Department’s 525, March 9, 7 p.m.) in transit through the Soviet
                    Union has been promised.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
